## Advanced Heap Problems & Techniques

One important problem is **K Closest Elements to a Given Value**. Given an array and a value X, we need to find the K elements closest to X. This can be solved using a Max Heap of size K. For each element, calculate its distance from X using `abs(arr[i] - X)` and store `{distance, element}` in the heap. If the heap size exceeds K, remove the top element. The remaining K elements are the answer. The time complexity is **O(N log K)**.

Another common problem is **Frequency Sort**, where elements are sorted according to their frequency. First, count frequencies using a HashMap. Then store `{frequency, element}` pairs in a Max Heap and extract elements based on frequency. This approach takes **O(N log N)** time.

The **Connect Ropes with Minimum Cost** problem is a classic heap application. Given rope lengths, we must connect all ropes with the minimum possible cost. The optimal approach uses a Min Heap. Insert all rope lengths into the heap, repeatedly remove the two smallest ropes, add their lengths to the total cost, and insert the combined rope back into the heap. Continue until only one rope remains. The time complexity is **O(N log N)**.

In the **Sum Between K1-th and K2-th Smallest Elements** problem, we first find the K1-th and K2-th smallest elements and then calculate the sum of all elements strictly between them. Heaps allow efficient retrieval of Kth smallest elements, leading to a complexity of **O(N log K)**.

The **Median in a Stream** problem involves finding the median after each incoming number. This is solved using two heaps. A Max Heap stores the smaller half of the elements, while a Min Heap stores the larger half. The heaps are balanced so that their sizes differ by at most one. If both heaps have the same size, the median is the average of their top elements. Otherwise, the median is the top element of the larger heap. Each insertion takes **O(log N)** time. This technique is widely used in real-time analytics, financial systems, and streaming data applications.

Heaps are also heavily used in graph algorithms, especially **Dijkstra’s Algorithm**. Dijkstra repeatedly requires the node with the minimum distance from the source. By storing `{distance, node}` pairs in a Min Heap, the complexity improves from **O(V²)** to **O((V + E) log V)**, making it efficient for large graphs.

